    36	
    37	### Joint evaluation path
    38	
    39	For each anchor, one fresh nominal `StepEvaluator` with `boundary_indices=(0,)` received the canonical default configuration and every multi-user catalogue configuration in one `evaluate_many` call. No singleton configuration was sent to an evaluator. This matches the stored physics header’s declared current candidate/default path. Forecasts were not regenerated because C3 needs the immediate `Delta F(A)` only; the already-stored C2 forecast quantities were not changed.
    40	
    41	The stored unilateral term for each member was computed from its authenticated stored singleton and stored default. The new joint term was computed from the jointly evaluated configuration and the fresh default from that same joint batch. Thus neither `d_i` nor `Delta F(A)` mixes scalar/dense paths or boundary policies. Both use nominal `evaluate_many` at boundary `[0]`.
    42	
    43	A relevant implementation fact was exposed: a fresh default evaluated on the joint batch was not always binary-identical to the default stored from the original default-plus-singletons batch. The dense engine occupancy-sorts configurations and processes chunks ([run_v025_matrix_probe.py evaluator entry](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-coalgen-ws/.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/probe/run_v025_matrix_probe.py:793), [batch sorting/chunking](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-c1c2suff-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/batch.py:159)). The build therefore did not substitute either default across batches: stored `d_i` and fresh `Delta F(A)` each retain their own internally consistent default evaluation, and `Psi_A` is formed as the requested difference of those two measured increments.
