92:- Main boundary-0 selection is safe from mixing: the full catalogue, including BASE, is batch-populated before any `evaluate` reads (`run_v025_matrix_probe.py:2608-2639`).
94:- **Mixed reporting can occur for S_UNI.** `unique_selected` is formed while S_UNI still equals BASE; the independently selected S_UNI is installed later and called via `evaluate`, which becomes a scalar full-48 miss when it is not BASE or another pre-batched arm (`:2742`, `:2766-2769`, `:2786-2805`). Its arm receipt is then pooled beside dense arms (`:2900-2927`, `:3068-3097`), and the merged `FULL_vs_S_UNI` diagnostic consumes the two (`:4606-4629`). This does not enter the three FULL-vs-DROP claim marginals, but it does affect the Level-A comparator. On Q1’s realised full-grid distribution, the rough numerical scale is ≤2.75×10⁻⁴ bit/config and ≤1.46×10⁻¹¹ J/config—not the multi-κ boundary-0 defect.
103:- **Ordinary primitive C1/C2 source-row labels are not mixed physical differences.** C1 and C2 are explicit κ-scaled gain-ratio surrogates (`:398-427`, `:460-462`). The scalar full-trajectory BASE nevertheless feeds features such as margin, power, and spectral efficiency (`:410-412`, `:431`, `:455-458`), so those features violate the stated boundary-0 intent. The disabled nonprimitive builder batches BASE and all action configs together before computing physical label differences (`:550-610`) and would be path-consistent.
104:- **Coalition training rows are mixed.** The primitive builder calls `current.evaluate_many((base, selected))`; BASE is skipped because it is scalar-cached, while the pairwise selected row is dense boundary-0 (`:501-514`). `_coalition_row` then combines scalar reference and scalar-on-miss singleton outcomes with the dense selected outcome (`:910-939`). The joint term’s reference-offset error is the boundary-0 BASE gap; ψ additionally mixes scalar singletons with a dense joint.
105:- **Pilot evaluation’s exact boundary-0 selectors are mixed.** `_exact_s0_boundary0` batch-populates candidates but retains scalar BASE, then ranks raw bits (`:1458-1500`). The call into `_s_uni_select` reuses that evaluator (`:1638-1652`); `_s_uni_select` reads BASE/current with `evaluate` and batches candidate neighborhoods (`run_v025_matrix_probe.py:1831-1924`), so its first sweep compares scalar BASE with dense candidates. `_exact_interactions` similarly batches catalogues/singletons and subtracts its cached anchor (`run_v025_pilot_c3.py:1328-1379`); P-a0 uses scalar BASE as that anchor. These can alter S0/S_UNI selection, joint deltas, and ψ—not merely reporting.
106:- At the eight sampled real BASE anchors, the dense-minus-scalar bit gap was −13.2449 to +21.2834 κ. Including the pilot’s η-priced energy term, the exact constant error injected into a dense-candidate minus scalar-BASE objective ranged from −17.2608 to +28.6520 κ. This is the appropriate rough corruption scale for mixed source/selection differences. It is not an EE percentage.
107:- **Pilot realised reports are path-consistent.** A fresh realised full-grid evaluator batch-populates selected arms before reads (`run_v025_pilot_c3.py:1678-1727`), and reporting singletons are also explicitly batched before the matched decomposition (`:1767-1805`). The reported pooled EE summary uses those realised bits/joules (`:1869-1894`). Selection may already have been corrupted upstream, but the endpoint subtraction itself is not mixed.
